I updated `router.py` so that PIN messages go to cards, and I wrote `NOTES.md`. **No automated test ran.** Each Python run needed approval, and this session could not give it, so I checked all 32 evidence rows by hand.

**→ The new rule.** If Laya answers `account_security` and the message has the word **"PIN"**, the answer becomes `cards`. The match ignores case and also catches "pins", "pincode" and "pin-code". Word edges stop "spin", "pinned" and "opinion" from matching.

**→ Why only account_security changes.** The correction says PIN questions belong to cards "not account_security", and all **16 wrong rows** are that switch. A declined payment that only mentions a PIN still goes to disputes.

**→ The exchange-rate rule from step 1 is the same as before.** It still runs first. The two rules act on different Laya answers, so they cannot conflict.

**→ Hand check: 32 / 32 rows match.** The two account_security rows with no "PIN" (one about the passcode, one about identity verification) stay in account_security.

**→ One known risk.** If a customer calls the app passcode their "PIN", the router sends the message to cards.

**Next step:** run the test script I wrote and confirm 32/32 plus the 8 extra test messages: `python3 -I <tmp> $WSROOT/laya/s1/opus-high-code-r0/step2`
